## On Part i (Open Problem)

Part i asks whether $\gcd(a_n, L_n) = 1$ for infinitely many $n$. This is a genuine open problem. The Steinerberger characterization tells us exactly *when* $p \mid \gcd(a_n, L_n)$: it depends on whether $p \mid \operatorname{num}(H_k)$ where $k$ is the leading digit of $n$ in base $p$. For $\gcd(a_n, L_n) = 1$, we need that for *every* prime $p \leq n$, the leading digit $k_p$ of $n$ in base $p$ satisfies $p \nmid \operatorname{num}(H_{k_p})$.

The heuristic (Shiu [Sh16]) predicts $\asymp x/\log x$ such $n$ up to $x$, arising from the probabilistic independence of the leading digits across different primes. However, converting this heuristic into a proof requires controlling correlations between the conditions for different primes, which has not been achieved. **I cannot solve this open problem.**

